WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.960
Part eight: the audit, taken further. Each line against several earlier years, the G S T returns against the books, the Form 3 C D checklist in order of risk, and the statutory audit with the Accounting Standards.

2
00:00:16.561 --> 00:00:28.821
Sri Venkateswara Traders now has three earlier years beside this one - two from their own statements, and last year from the previous-year column. Each year is its own column.

3
00:00:29.453 --> 00:00:43.833
Rent was about three point one lakh for three years, and is six lakh now - while turnover grew eight percent. Up sharply on last year, and above its usual range: it has broken its own pattern.

4
00:00:44.453 --> 00:00:54.843
A line that grows with the business year after year is following its pattern, and is not flagged. One click raises the rent as an audit point.

5
00:00:55.467 --> 00:01:08.187
Next, the G S T returns. Add the G S T R 3 B and G S T R 1 files from the G S T portal - the JSON, or the zip it downloads as.

6
00:01:08.796 --> 00:01:22.646
Month by month, G S T R 3 B against G S T R 1. In May, G S T R 1 is two and a half lakh higher; March's G S T R 3 B was never filed.

7
00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:37.170
And the year against the books: turnover in the books is about thirty five lakh higher than the G S T R 3 B - sales not declared, or income outside G S T. That too becomes an audit point.

8
00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:45.510
The Form 3 C D checklist, now in order of risk. Pick the line of business - a trader.

9
00:01:46.125 --> 00:02:14.925
Each clause shows high, medium or low, and why. Clause 22, payments to small suppliers, comes first: creditors jumped, and it often goes wrong in trading. Loans in cash, because unsecured loans tripled. Clause 21 b, payments without T D S, because rent and interest are flagged. As new statements come in, the order changes by itself.

10
00:02:15.546 --> 00:02:35.926
For a firm, an L L P, an A O P or a proprietor: the statutory audit. An L L P sees whether its accounts must be audited. Every entity gets its level under the I C A I scheme - here level four, an M S M E, from turnover and borrowings.

11
00:02:36.546 --> 00:02:52.166
Then the Accounting Standards that apply at that level - some in full, some with relaxations - and the ones that do not apply at this level, kept apart. Each is worked through like a clause; an issue opens an audit point.

12
00:02:52.765 --> 00:03:02.845
Every point raised - from earlier years, from the G S T returns, from a clause or a standard - in one list, each with who answers it.

13
00:03:03.467 --> 00:03:23.577
The limits, the flags, how far back to look, where each figure sits in a G S T return, how risky each clause is, and how each standard applies at each level are all SimplyFile's own rules, kept as chips - change one, and every client follows. Thank you for watching.
